```c
int counter = 0;

void* increment(void* arg) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {
        counter++;          // NOT atomic — read, add, write
    }
    return NULL;
}

int main() {
    pthread_t t1, t2;
    pthread_create(&t1, NULL, increment, NULL);
    pthread_create(&t2, NULL, increment, NULL);
    pthread_join(t1, NULL);
    pthread_join(t2, NULL);
    printf("%d\n", counter);   // expected 2000000, prints something less
    return 0;
}
```

Compile this without any locking and run it a few times: you'll see different numbers each run, all of them at or below 2,000,000, never above. That gap — sometimes tens of thousands of lost increments on a real multi-core box — is the race made visible. It compiles cleanly, runs without crashing, and is simply wrong.
